= Project and Organizational Structure
== Organization and System View of Project Management

An organization is a group of people with different knowledge, skills, and responsibilities who work together to achieve common organizational goals. Projects cannot be run in isolation. They must operate within the broader organizational environment. Therefore, a project manager needs to take a holistic or system view of the project and understand how the project is connected to and affected by the larger organization.

=== System View of Project Management
The System View of Project Management means looking at a project as a complete system, where different parts work together to achieve project objectives. A Project Management System is an organized set of tools, processes, people, and procedures that helps project managers and teams achieve project goals. It focuses on how people, processes, tools, and resources interact with each other.

*Importance:*
- Understand the project as a whole.
- Coordinate different parts of the project effectively.
- Understand how changes in one part can affect other parts.
- Use resources efficiently.
- Achieve project goals successfully.

=== Three Parts of Project System View
+ *Systems Philosophy:* Understanding the project as a system of connected and interacting components working together within an environment to achieve objectives (*Simple meaning:* Look at the whole project, not just individual parts).
+ *Systems Analysis:* A systematic problem-solving approach used to identify the problems, requirements, and possible solutions (*Simple meaning:* Understand the problem properly and find the best solution).
+ *Systems Management:* Deals with the business, technological, and organizational aspects of a project before implementing changes. It ensures that the system operates effectively and efficiently (*Simple meaning:* Manage all aspects of the system properly before and after making changes).

=== Three Sphere Model for Systems Management (BOT)
The Three Sphere Model explains the three main areas that must be considered for the successful management of an ICT project.

+ *Business Sphere*
  - Focuses on the business goals and objectives of the project.
  - Ensures the project provides value to the organization.
  - Considers factors such as cost, profit, and business requirements.

+ *Organizational Sphere*
  - Focuses on people and organizational issues.
  - Includes stakeholders, project teams, management, communication, and organizational culture.
  - Ensures people are properly involved and coordinated.

+ *Technological Sphere*
  - Deals with the technology required for the project.
  - Includes hardware, software, networks, databases, and other technologies.
  - Ensures the required technology is suitable for the project.

